WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.320
This AI plays Doom.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.740
Its answer isn't a
paragraph.

3
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.320
It's the next move.

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.140
Let me show you how a
game

5
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.560
situation becomes a
decision.

6
00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:13.909
Pause there.

7
00:00:13.909 --> 00:00:16.929
The useful input is the
game state:

8
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:18.929
things like health,
ammunition

9
00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:20.509
and what's nearby.

10
00:00:20.509 --> 00:00:22.029
That's information the
program

11
00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:24.408
can pass into a decision.

12
00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:27.148
Add a goal and a set of
allowed moves.

13
00:00:27.148 --> 00:00:29.888
Then ask one precise
question:

14
00:00:29.888 --> 00:00:32.368
which move fits this
situation?

15
00:00:32.368 --> 00:00:34.328
The recording's
instruction says:

16
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:37.649
don't fire, simply dodge.

17
00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:41.209
In simplified code, read
the state,

18
00:00:41.209 --> 00:00:43.529
ask for a decision, then
let the

19
00:00:43.529 --> 00:00:46.409
game apply the selected
action.

20
00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:49.089
The model chooses; the
surrounding

21
00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:51.569
program does the work.

22
00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:53.619
Back in the recording,
movement

23
00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.299
and firing have separate
outputs.

24
00:00:56.299 --> 00:00:58.979
Follow the movement route
to MOVE.

25
00:00:58.979 --> 00:01:01.359
But firing continues.

26
00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:03.439
Choosing a move doesn't
mean the

27
00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:06.219
whole instruction was
obeyed.

28
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.550
A language model normally
generates

29
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.610
its response piece by
piece, even when

30
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.090
that response is
structured data.

31
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.030
TypeSafe's Jev is built
to return typed decisions

32
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.233
directly, without
generating that text.

33
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:24.153
TypeSafe calls this a
System One model.

34
00:01:24.153 --> 00:01:25.753
Choice selects an option.

35
00:01:25.753 --> 00:01:27.793
Score rates a defined
scale.

36
00:01:27.793 --> 00:01:30.813
Noul estimates the
probability of yes.

37
00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:34.904
Independent questions can
be asked together.

38
00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:36.644
TypeSafe reports
responses

39
00:01:36.644 --> 00:01:38.324
in half a second or less.

40
00:01:38.324 --> 00:01:40.384
That's its published
range, not

41
00:01:40.384 --> 00:01:43.024
a stopwatch on this
recording.

42
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.660
That's the catch:

43
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.120
a valid answer can still
be the wrong move.

44
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.740
Your program must check
the result

45
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.360
against its rules before
acting, and

46
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.445
decide when to stop or
ask a person.

47
00:01:57.445 --> 00:01:59.725
There's also Open-Jev:

48
00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:01.645
independent open-source

49
00:02:01.645 --> 00:02:04.365
work inspired by the
idea.

50
00:02:04.365 --> 00:02:06.585
It's not TypeSafe's
model, and the

51
00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:08.825
same performance isn't
promised.

52
00:02:08.825 --> 00:02:12.305
Keep language models for
explanations.

53
00:02:12.305 --> 00:02:14.025
Use a decision model
where

54
00:02:14.025 --> 00:02:16.935
your code needs a choice.

55
00:02:16.935 --> 00:02:20.095
Comment JEV and I'll send
you the guide.
